WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
I was just telling my son that very

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:06.160
thing this last week and he's having a

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.120
tough time trying to figure out where

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.840
his interests lie, where what he wants

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:12.200
to do, how he's going to

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.960
you know, he he's 18.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:18.320
And he just struggles with the reality

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.560
of the way job market is. He just

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.400
doesn't feel like he's like innately

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.720
like ready to go and just do something

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.160
accomplish something. So he's in school,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.840
he's trying to figure it out. I tell him

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.920
it's 18 you're 18 years old, no one's

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.720
kicking you out of the house, you know,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.400
tomorrow or anything like that.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.200
But [snorts]

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.320
>> Has he got a taste of he's he got a

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.640
taste of

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.320
you know, going to find it in his own

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.720
customer and making 30 bucks an hour

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:41.320
while all of his friends are making you

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.280
know, 15 or $16 an hour at their jobs?

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:47.360
>> He he hasn't had that yet. He he ran

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.840
when he was 14 years old, he he ran a

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.960
booth at the

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.400
the farmers market for a local company

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.240
and that really pushed him because he

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.200
had to get out of you know, his shell in

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.360
terms of being able to talk to strangers

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.960
and handle transactions and balance the

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.480
accounts when he was done and everything

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.000
like that. And so I think he sees it but

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.280
ultimately he's just trying to figure

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.960
out, you know, what is what is he going

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.000
to do and how can he create value in the

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.200
world and be rewarded for it. I

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.800
definitely want him to read your book.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.920
Um

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.760
but I was just telling him the whole

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.800
thing is like you you can't sit around

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:21.080
waiting for anyone to either give you

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.200
direction or give you permission. Like

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.240
it has to come from with inside you so

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.560
that you can move forward doing it and I

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.200
mean you've been a

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:28.880
a great example of of making that

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.440
happen.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.400
>> I think the best thing that you could do

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.960
for him is to get him a taste of sending

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.000
that first invoice. Like when I got that

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.920
first I was 14 when my dad who's just

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.800
like me, he's just irrationally

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:43.320
optimistic at times which is a blessing

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.680
and a curse for us Hubers because we

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.960
bite off more than we can chew a lot of

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:49.360
times. But he

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.560
a contractor in Tell City

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.240
Indiana where I grew up was mowing lawns

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.800
for my dad's boss who owned a couple

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.320
shopping centers. Well, the guy had a

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.920
heart attack and couldn't keep mowing.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.000
He was fine but he just couldn't keep

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.400
mowing. And my dad's bright idea was to

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.080
walk in and tell his boss that his

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.560
14-year-old son could take over these

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.920
jobs. And it was like

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.600
uh I don't know, $400 a week of mowing

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.160
across like six shopping centers and an

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.480
apartment complex.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.160
And um

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.640
like that first day on the job, I wanted

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.640
like I was like, "What am I doing here?"

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.959
I was hot. It was 95°. I was mowing

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.200
through trash. There were cars ripping

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:21.400
by.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.600
And if if you're listening to this, it's

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.400
like on the main drag on, you know, in

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.400
front of the movie theater in Tell City.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.959
Like that's the lawn I mowed. You can

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.400
see it on Google Maps. And I had no

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:34.640
business doing that. I was crying at

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.240
halfway through the job. I'm like, "Dad,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.760
like I want to go home. I'm 14. This is

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:39.040
 crazy."

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.400
And he goes, all right, he put his arm

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.400
around me, put a gave me a Gatorade, and

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.280
put me in the air conditioning of his

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.480
truck and said, "Nick, you can quit. You

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.360
can quit.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.920
But not until you send your first

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.680
invoice."

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.040
And I never quit because I got that

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.440
invoice and did some basic math.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.600
And like at the time, my girlfriend was

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.720
working at Subway making $6.25 an hour

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:01.160
with no tips.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.400
And I could make I could make $28 an

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.600
hour after fuel and everything on that

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.920
lawn mower.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.120
And I thought about the world in a whole

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.760
new way, and I haven't had an hourly job

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.760
ever since.

